#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Wave 43: Just Teens, a Fifth Time — the floor rises to nine,
and the Vault presents itself as what it now is: a live database.

Wave 42 closed by naming this wave: the 13/14 band was still
the thinnest in the vault at 112 against 160 at the young
end, its floor was 8, and stem and holidays stood tied at 10.
This wave's fresh audit measured the whole archive again
before anything was built. Per-age coverage at wave 42's
close: 3: 160, 4: 160, 5: 329, 6: 426, 7: 496, 8: 530,
9: 524, 10: 518, 11: 456, 12: 446, 13: 112, 14: 112.
Inside the band, the primary-category cells measure
seasons 8, outdoor 8, pe-movement 8, music-rhythm 8,
rainy-day 8, icebreakers 9, crafts 9, stem 10, holidays 10,
quiet-focus 15, team-community 19 — a floor of 8, shared
by five categories.

The wave's size is sized from those cells, the same way
waves 39-42 sized theirs: the eight thinnest cells in the
band are the five at the floor of 8, the two at 9, and one
of the two tied at 10. One record for each of those eight
cells is eight records — no more, no fewer. The tie at 10
is broken on the tag shelves, honestly and in the open,
the same way wave 41 broke its tie: the holidays record
carries the St. Patrick's Day tag, which stands at 6
overall — one of the six floor holiday tags — while no
STEM tag shelf is anywhere near as thin. Result, asserted
in the wave log: total 739 -> 747; age-13 coverage
112 -> 120 and age-14 coverage 112 -> 120, every other age
unchanged; the 13/14 primary floor rises 8 -> 9 (every
category in the band now carries at least 9 primaries);
13-14-only records 36 -> 44; and eight honest picks meet
the build validator's 8-12 collection rule, so the 32nd
collection — "Just Teens, a Fifth Time" — unlocks at
exactly the 8-pick minimum with no padding and no lowered
bar. The remaining gap to the 160 benchmark is 40 at each
age, stated numerically, not spun.

Deliberate neighbours, kept on purpose and named here:
- growing-degree-day-ledger vs adopt-a-tree-year-log (a
  year watched at one tree) and one-branch-bud-count
  (age 3, buds counted by fingertip): the ledger does not
  watch a plant, it PREDICTS one — daily temperatures are
  converted to growing degree-days, a budburst prediction
  is entered in writing before the event, and the season
  grades the prediction in numbers.
- microclimate-map-survey vs transect-line-field-survey
  (wave 41, life counted along one fixed line) and
  weather-journal (the sky logged day by day): the survey
  maps ONE PLACE at ONE HOUR — temperature, shade and wind
  read at a grid of stations simultaneously, drawn as a
  map of the many climates inside a single yard.
- kin-ball-first-session vs tchoukball-first-session
  (wave 42, shots rebounded off a frame): kin-ball has no
  frame, no goal and THREE teams at once; the ball is
  enormous, the serve is a spoken call naming a colour,
  and a team wins ground by being named and NOT holding
  the ball up — cooperation between rivals is the tactic.
- conducting-patterns-studio vs part-singing-choir-first-
  rehearsals (wave 41, singers holding lines) and song-
  arranging-studio-first-session (wave 42, a song rebuilt):
  the studio teaches the CONDUCTOR's craft — beat patterns
  in 2, 3 and 4, cueing, starting and stopping a room —
  with the ensemble as the instrument the conductor plays.
- one-table-micro-museum vs public-art-proposal (art
  proposed for a public place) and masterpiece-coloring-
  gallery (colouring hung as a gallery): the museum is
  CURATION — real objects loaned by their owners, labelled
  in the owner's own facts, arranged into one argument on
  one table, and returned entire.
- sashiko-stitch-sampler-studio vs kente-pattern-strips
  (woven paper strips) and pamphlet-stitch-bookbinding-
  studio (wave 42, thread through a spine): sashiko is a
  running stitch worked through cloth in counted rows —
  the thread decorates and strengthens the surface itself,
  and the sampler is a pattern library, not a binding.
- personal-map-introductions vs partner-portrait-
  interviews (wave 41, one deep spoken interview) and
  spectrum-line-introductions (wave 42, feet answering
  along a line): the map is DRAWN first — the places that
  made you, at whatever scale is honest — and the
  introduction is a guided tour of a country only its
  drawer knows, with whole regions lawfully left blank.
- celtic-knot-drawing-studio vs ornament-geometry-stars
  (five-point star geometry at Christmas) and rangoli-
  pattern-art (pattern laid for Diwali): the knot is ONE
  unbroken line woven over and under itself on a dot grid,
  drawn for St. Patrick's Day — the discipline is the
  weave's alternation, checked crossing by crossing.

Two drafted records were cut before the pack ran, on the
standing near-dupe discipline: a solstice shadow
comparison chart for the seasons slot — winter-solstice-
shadow-track and longest-day-shadow-track already track
shadows at both ends of the year, and a third shadow
chart is the same instrument pointed at a third date —
and a compass-points introduction for the icebreakers
slot — spectrum-line-introductions (wave 42) already
answers with the body placed along a line, and a compass
rose is the same body-vote bent into a circle. Both slots
were rebuilt from genuinely empty ground: no degree-day
ledger and no drawn personal map existed on the shelf.

The drama/coding lane, recorded not padded (standing
rule): still no drama or coding category among the
eleven, and no validator tag names one. This wave does
not invent a twelfth category to look complete, and it
does not bolt a coding record onto any slot — the STEM
cell was not among the eight thinnest once the tie broke
on the tag shelves, and it is left for a wave briefed to
build it properly.

Safety notes are practical only — no medical claims anywhere.

Run once: dedupes by id, writes data/activities.json and
data/collections.json. Run scripts/build.py afterwards.
"""
